 The built-in MCP support connects registered servers. When nothing does, because another extension replaced it (see [MCP](mcp.md#other-mcp-extensions)), each registration is reported as an extension error. Other MCP extensions can connect registered servers too: read them with `pi.getMcpServers()` on `session_start` and handle the `mcp_servers_change` event for later changes.
 
+### Manage the built-in MCP owner
+
+A patched Pi 1.2.0 MCP extension also exposes its session-scoped management handle through the public `pi.events` bus. Use the exported protocol constants and types to negotiate the version and required capabilities; do not reach into the built-in extension's implementation or create another MCP client. The handle provides immutable redacted snapshots, immediate-and-live subscriptions, enable/disable, reconnect, server/tool exposure, catalog refresh, and native OAuth operations. Every action returns a structured result, and the handle becomes unusable when the session ends. The unmodified Pi 1.2.0 release has no management protocol; see [MCP](mcp.md#manage-built-in-mcp-connections-from-another-extension).
