 ### Configuration rules
@@ -97,7 +101,11 @@ Run `pi mcp list` to connect to every enabled server and print its state, tools,
 
 Pi reports configuration errors, failed connections, and required sign-ins once after startup. Server logging notifications are appended to `~/.pi/agent/mcp.log` as `<time> [<server>] <level> <logger>: <message>`. The file moves to `mcp.log.1` after it grows past 5 MB.
 
-Pi connects every enabled server in the background when a session starts. A server's tools appear once it connects; the `codemode` description does not list them, so it does not change when servers connect. The first prompt waits up to 10 seconds only for servers with `direct` tools, which must be declared in its request. Other servers are waited for when they are needed: a codemode script waits for the servers it names (`mcp__<server>`) and, when it calls `searchTools()` or reads `ALL_TOOLS`, for all of them; `tool_search` and the resource tools also wait for all of them. HTTP network errors and transient statuses (408, 429, and 5xx) are retried twice. A dropped connection is shown as disconnected and reconnects on the next call. When a server announces a changed tool list, new tools are added and withdrawn tools become unreachable.
+Without `lifecycle`, Pi connects every enabled server in the background when a session starts, as before. With lifecycle management enabled, `eager` connects at startup and is reclaimed after the idle timeout; `keep-alive` also connects at startup and reconnects a dropped connection without idle reclamation; `lazy` and `lazy-keep-alive` defer connection when a valid offline catalog seed exists. A lazy server used through `codemode` or `tool_search` connects when that tool first reaches it; cached metadata itself is never executable. Servers with `direct` tools still connect at startup because the model needs their live input schemas before it can call them. On a cold start or after the seed expires or no longer matches the server configuration, Pi keeps the existing bootstrap behavior and connects to refresh the catalog. The top-level idle timeout (default 10 minutes) applies to `eager` and `lazy` connections unless the server overrides it; zero disables reclamation. `lazy-keep-alive` becomes persistent after its first connection.
+
+Offline catalog seeds live in `~/.pi/agent/mcp-catalog-cache.json` (or the configured agent directory), expire after seven days, and are invalidated when catalog-affecting server configuration changes. They contain only tool names and descriptions plus a configuration hash and validity timestamps. They are display metadata only: cached entries never execute, and the file contains no client, input schema, credentials, or raw server configuration. Connection status remains separate from catalog freshness.
+
+A server's live tools appear once it connects; the `codemode` description does not list them, so it does not change when servers connect. The first prompt waits up to 10 seconds only for servers with `direct` tools, which must be declared in its request. Other servers are waited for when they are needed: a codemode script waits for the servers it names (`mcp__<server>`) and, when it calls `searchTools()` or reads `ALL_TOOLS`, for all of them; `tool_search` and the resource tools also wait for all of them. HTTP network errors and transient statuses (408, 429, and 5xx) are retried twice. A dropped connection is shown as disconnected and reconnects on the next call. When a server announces a changed tool list, new tools are added and withdrawn tools become unreachable.
 
 Stopping a stdio server closes its stdin, sends SIGTERM, then sends SIGKILL to its process group. This also stops servers launched through wrappers such as `npx` or `uvx`.
 
@@ -261,6 +269,14 @@ Extensions can add servers for the current session with `pi.registerMcpServer(na
 
 Changes to enabled state or exposure apply only to the current session. A file-configured server with the same name takes precedence, and `/mcp` lists the overridden registration. `pi mcp` shell commands do not load extensions and only see file-configured servers.
 
+### Manage built-in MCP connections from another extension
+
+The patched Pi 1.2.0 MCP extension exposes a typed, session-scoped management protocol through `pi.events`. It is available from the public coding-agent entry point as `MCP_MANAGEMENT_*` constants and `McpManagementHandle` types. A consumer listens for `MCP_MANAGEMENT_AVAILABLE_EVENT`, sends `MCP_MANAGEMENT_DISCOVERY_EVENT` with `protocol`, `version`, `requestId`, and `requiredCapabilities`, then listens on `${MCP_MANAGEMENT_RESPONSE_EVENT_PREFIX}${requestId}`. Discovery works even if the consumer missed the availability notification. Unsupported versions and missing required capabilities return structured errors without a handle.
+
+The handle exposes immutable, redacted snapshots, a cancellable subscription that immediately sends the current snapshot, and typed enable/disable, reconnect, exposure, catalog-refresh, and OAuth operations. Connection state is separate from catalog freshness: disabling retains the latest successful catalog as cached, but withdraws its tools and closes the connection. The snapshot contains no client, raw server configuration, credentials, transport errors, or local paths. All execution continues through the built-in MCP owner and its normal permission/tool pipeline; an extension using this protocol does not replace `/mcp` or create another MCP runtime. Handles expire at `session_shutdown` and cannot be reused in a later session.
+
+The unmodified Pi 1.2.0 release does not include this protocol; it requires the corresponding source patch. The adapter repository maintains its consumer contract in `docs/native-mcp-protocol.md`.
+
 ### Replace the built-in MCP support
 
 An installed extension that registers `/mcp`, such as `pi-mcp-adapter`, replaces the built-in MCP support for sessions. Pi then does not read `mcp.json` or connect its servers in a session, and `/mcp` belongs to the extension. Remove the extension to restore the built-in behavior. To disable built-in MCP support without a replacement, disable `mcp` under Built-in in `pi config`, or set `"extensions": ["-builtin:mcp"]` in [settings](settings.md#resources). `--no-mcp` disables it for one run.
